1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,567
By twenty thirty-five,

2
00:00:01,607 --> 00:00:06,499
America is expected to have almost six
million more jobs than today.

3
00:00:06,539 --> 00:00:09,065
But some jobs are going the other way.

4
00:00:09,105 --> 00:00:09,267
Fast.

5
00:00:09,307 --> 00:00:13,393
A few could lose a third of their workers
in just ten years.

6
00:00:13,893 --> 00:00:17,459
Here are ten jobs that are disappearing
fastest,

7
00:00:17,499 --> 00:00:20,544
ranked by the official projections of the
U.S.

8
00:00:20,584 --> 00:00:21,772
Bureau of Labor Statistics.

9
00:00:21,812 --> 00:00:23,265
Not overnight.

10
00:00:23,305 --> 00:00:26,777
But year by year, there will be fewer of
them.

11
00:00:27,277 --> 00:00:28,479
Number ten.

12
00:00:28,519 --> 00:00:30,665
The sewing machine operator.

13
00:00:30,705 --> 00:00:34,720
About a hundred and seventeen thousand
Americans still sew clothes,

14
00:00:34,760 --> 00:00:37,705
bags and furniture covers in factories.

15
00:00:38,205 --> 00:00:39,924
By twenty thirty-five,

16
00:00:39,964 --> 00:00:42,934
that number is expected to drop by about
fifteen percent.

17
00:00:42,974 --> 00:00:46,908
Most clothing is now made overseas,

18
00:00:46,948 --> 00:00:49,545
and machines handle more of the simple
seams.

19
00:00:50,045 --> 00:00:51,143
Number nine.

20
00:00:51,183 --> 00:00:51,953
The camera repairer.

21
00:00:51,993 --> 00:00:55,777
There are fewer than two thousand of them
left in the whole country.

22
00:00:56,277 --> 00:01:01,101
And they are expected to shrink by fifteen
and a half percent.

23
00:01:01,141 --> 00:01:03,655
Most photos are now taken on phones.

24
00:01:03,695 --> 00:01:07,107
And when a camera breaks, people usually
just replace it.

25
00:01:07,607 --> 00:01:08,624
Number eight.

26
00:01:08,664 --> 00:01:10,260
The file clerk.

27
00:01:10,300 --> 00:01:12,904
Their job is to keep paper records in
order,

28
00:01:12,944 --> 00:01:15,232
so anyone can find the right folder.

29
00:01:15,732 --> 00:01:18,471
But paper is moving into computers.

30
00:01:18,511 --> 00:01:23,668
A search box finds in one second what used
to take a clerk ten minutes.

31
00:01:23,708 --> 00:01:27,848
Expected change: almost sixteen percent
fewer file clerks.

32
00:01:28,348 --> 00:01:29,153
Number seven.

33
00:01:29,193 --> 00:01:31,538
The carpet installer.

34
00:01:31,578 --> 00:01:35,732
This one is a surprise, because people
still buy homes and renovate them.

35
00:01:36,232 --> 00:01:39,583
The difference is the floor itself.

36
00:01:39,623 --> 00:01:45,309
Many homeowners now choose wood, tile or
vinyl instead of wall-to-wall carpet.

37
00:01:45,349 --> 00:01:48,107
Expected drop: about sixteen percent.

38
00:01:48,607 --> 00:01:49,346
Number six.

39
00:01:49,386 --> 00:01:51,811
The print binding worker.

40
00:01:51,851 --> 00:01:57,196
They fold, stitch, glue and trim pages
into books, magazines and catalogs.

41
00:01:57,696 --> 00:02:03,279
Fewer things are printed today, and modern
binding machines need fewer hands.

42
00:02:03,319 --> 00:02:06,062
Expected drop: seventeen and a half
percent.

43
00:02:06,562 --> 00:02:07,377
Number five.

44
00:02:07,417 --> 00:02:08,871
The telemarketer.

45
00:02:08,911 --> 00:02:13,071
The person who calls you at dinner time to
sell you something.

46
00:02:13,571 --> 00:02:16,592
Most people no longer answer unknown
numbers,

47
00:02:16,632 --> 00:02:20,820
and many sales calls are now made by
automated systems.

48
00:02:20,860 --> 00:02:24,482
About one in five telemarketing jobs is
expected to vanish.

49
00:02:24,982 --> 00:02:25,786
Number four.

50
00:02:25,826 --> 00:02:27,079
The foundry mold maker.

51
00:02:27,119 --> 00:02:32,968
They shape the sand molds that hot liquid
metal is poured into,

52
00:02:33,008 --> 00:02:35,821
to make engine parts, pipes and machine
pieces.

53
00:02:36,321 --> 00:02:38,980
More of this work is now done by machines,

54
00:02:39,020 --> 00:02:42,229
including printers that print the molds
directly.

55
00:02:42,269 --> 00:02:44,839
Expected drop: twenty-three percent.

56
00:02:45,339 --> 00:02:46,528
Number three.

57
00:02:46,568 --> 00:02:48,392
The data entry keyer.

58
00:02:48,432 --> 00:02:53,069
They type information from forms, invoices
and records into computers,

59
00:02:53,109 --> 00:02:53,929
all day long.

60
00:02:54,429 --> 00:02:59,157
Today, scanners and software read most of
those forms automatically.

61
00:02:59,197 --> 00:03:03,238
That's why a quarter of these jobs are
expected to disappear.

62
00:03:03,278 --> 00:03:04,911
About thirty-four thousand of them.

63
00:03:05,411 --> 00:03:06,580
Number two.

64
00:03:06,620 --> 00:03:08,237
The switchboard operator.

65
00:03:08,277 --> 00:03:12,357
They answer calls and connect each caller
to the right person or department.

66
00:03:12,857 --> 00:03:15,884
Now an automated menu does that.

67
00:03:15,924 --> 00:03:18,100
Press one for sales, press two for
support.

68
00:03:18,140 --> 00:03:22,913
Switchboard jobs are expected to fall by
twenty-six percent,

69
00:03:22,953 --> 00:03:26,241
and classic telephone operators by almost
twenty-eight.

70
00:03:26,741 --> 00:03:27,562
Number one.

71
00:03:27,602 --> 00:03:29,834
The word processor and typist.

72
00:03:29,874 --> 00:03:35,759
Their whole job is typing up letters,
reports and documents for other people.

73
00:03:36,259 --> 00:03:39,858
But today almost everyone types their own
emails and documents,

74
00:03:39,898 --> 00:03:42,106
and voice typing does the rest.

75
00:03:42,146 --> 00:03:45,798
Expected drop: more than thirty-four
percent.

76
00:03:45,838 --> 00:03:47,741
That's one in every three jobs.

77
00:03:48,241 --> 00:03:50,847
From about forty thousand typists today,

78
00:03:50,887 --> 00:03:54,286
to about twenty-six thousand in twenty
thirty-five.

79
00:03:54,786 --> 00:03:57,130
So what do these jobs have in common?

80
00:03:57,170 --> 00:04:02,178
Most of them repeat one task all day:
typing, filing, connecting calls,

81
00:04:02,218 --> 00:04:04,197
sewing the same seam.

82
00:04:04,237 --> 00:04:08,205
And a repeated task is exactly what
machines and software do best.

83
00:04:08,705 --> 00:04:10,906
But notice what is not happening.

84
00:04:10,946 --> 00:04:13,475
These jobs are not vanishing overnight.

85
00:04:13,515 --> 00:04:18,489
Tens of thousands of people still do them
today.

86
00:04:18,529 --> 00:04:22,698
And their skills, like precision, patience
and dealing with people,

87
00:04:22,738 --> 00:04:24,545
carry over to new jobs.

88
00:04:25,045 --> 00:04:29,525
So if your job is on this list, it's not a
reason to panic.

89
00:04:29,565 --> 00:04:33,902
It's a reason to learn the next skill a
little earlier than everyone else.

90
00:04:34,402 --> 00:04:38,667
Which of these jobs did you think had
already disappeared?

91
00:04:38,707 --> 00:04:39,488
Tell us in the comments.

92
00:04:39,528 --> 00:04:42,351
And if you liked this, subscribe.

93
00:04:42,391 --> 00:04:45,530
Next time: the new jobs that will appear
in the next ten years.

